[%# An application profile rather than a bare `ufw allow`.  setup-ufw-rules
    opens with `ufw reset`, which throws away every rule on the guest -- and the
    ufw target runs after this one, so a rule added here was deleted a few
    targets later.  Profiles live in /etc/ufw/applications.d, which the reset
    does not touch, so what is declared here is what setup-ufw-rules allows when
    it walks `ufw app list`.

    Only the configured port.  slapd ships its own OpenLDAP LDAP and LDAPS
    profiles, which is why the default of 636 went on working by accident; a
    domain that set port: to anything else had nothing opening it at all.

    Named slapd and not ldap, because ufw refuses a profile whose section name
    is also a service name in /etc/services and says so only as a warning on
    stderr:

        WARN: Skipping .ldap.: also in /etc/services

    -- so the profile is absent from `ufw app list`, no rule is ever made from
    it, and nothing fails.  The file is still called ldap: the slapd package
    owns /etc/ufw/applications.d/slapd, which is where its own two profiles
    live, and that is not ours to write over. -%]
[slapd]
title=LDAP directory
description=OpenLDAP directory server, on the port this domain configured
ports=[% port %]/tcp
